<title>MEXICO: Truck strike delays Mexico coffee exports slightly.</title>
<headline>Truck strike delays Mexico coffee exports slightly.</headline>
<dateline>MEXICO CITY 1997-02-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>A nationwide truck driver strike last week caused a few delays but no major disruption to Mexican coffee exports, Chiapas coffee trader Ernesto Gutierrez told Reuters Thursday.</p>
<p>Some 40,000 independent truck drivers went on strike last week demanding that the government boost highway security, cut red-tape and end police corruption. The truckers went back to work after three days off the roads.</p>
<p>&quot;It was lifted sooner than we had feared,&quot; Gutierrez said, saying crop deliveries saw slight delays.</p>
<p>Not all coffee traders use the independent trucking service, but those that do said they were afraid to replace the truckers on threat of violence.</p>
<p>Cold weather last week, however, may have caused damage to the remaining 25 percent of coffee crops yet to be harvested in key coffee state Chiapas, he said.</p>
<p>&quot;We will not know the damage from the cold until the end of the season,&quot; Gutierrez said.</p>
<p>Low temperatures can affect coffee trees by slowing down growth for upcoming cycles, although frost is much more damaging and has longer term impacts.</p>
<p>((--Timna Tanners, Mexico City newsroom +525 728-7903))</p>
